Management summary — London reverse mentoring session

Participants valued the firm’s supportive culture, access to senior colleagues and early responsibility. The main frustrations concerned technology, complex processes, uneven development guidance and limited visibility of strategy in some teams. The request was to preserve the benefits of a smaller European platform while improving the infrastructure and communication supporting it.

This summary is anonymised and based solely on the supplied transcript. Feedback reflects participants’ experiences; several issues were particularly focused on SPG and should not be treated as firm-wide findings.

What works well

* Supportive, inclusive culture. Participants described feeling comfortable being themselves, asking questions and putting forward ideas. Respect, recognition and colleagues’ willingness to teach were repeatedly highlighted.
* Early responsibility and client exposure. Junior staff valued owning meaningful work and gaining experience they believed would come later at larger teams elsewhere.
* Access to experienced colleagues. Informal teaching, direct access to seniors and the ability to brainstorm across teams were important strengths.
* Internal mobility. A participant’s move across geography and from middle office into front office illustrated how mobility can support retention and bring valuable operational knowledge into the business.
* Effective global connections in some areas. CDF was cited as a positive example of shared responsibilities, training resources and collaboration with US colleagues. Participants also described improving connectivity in ABS.
* Practical benefits from AI. Document summaries, explanations and routine formatting were helping staff learn independently and spend more time on higher-value work.
* Clear communication where it happens. One team described receiving prompt explanations of decisions, their implications and individual responsibilities. This was a useful example for others to follow.

What is not working consistently

Area	Points raised and concerns	Ideas for management consideration
Technology and support	Slow systems, cumbersome ticketing and unclear ownership of improvements. Staff often rely on personal contacts to resolve issues.	Publish clear support contacts and escalation routes; simplify requests; give users visibility of progress and outcomes.
Access to deal information	Participants reported delays releasing password-protected files and having to repeat access processes after permissions expire.	Review the workflow with Technology and control functions to reduce avoidable delays while maintaining required controls.
European–US processes	European teams covering several businesses must navigate different US processes and execution-team responsibilities. Confusion and response times can delay execution.	Clarify responsibilities by business, standardise common steps where appropriate and distinguish global requirements from processes needing regional flexibility.
Cross-business collaboration	Silos can make it harder to offer clients coordinated solutions across advisory, financing, trading and distribution.	Strengthen cross-team connections and consider client needs across the full range of capabilities before settling on a product solution.
Career progression	Some staff lacked clarity on grade expectations, promotion requirements and whether they were progressing well. Experience varied by manager, team and entry route.	Provide clearer expectations by grade, regular development conversations and specific feedback, while allowing for individual learning paths.
Training and onboarding	Learning relies heavily on colleagues’ availability. Some useful training materials already exist but are not consistently shared or easy to find.	Create a central onboarding resource with worked examples, source guidance and available US training materials; combine this with practical responsibility and coaching.
Approved languages	A participant reported a prolonged effort to obtain Greek-language approval, limiting communication with Greek clients through approved channels.	Establish a clear decision owner, approval criteria and status updates. Assess commercial demand, surveillance requirements and cost.
Strategy and communication	SPG participants described uncertainty about growth priorities and future capabilities. This affects confidence, career planning and consistency of client messaging.	Communicate what is decided, what remains under review, what teams can tell clients and when the next update will come.

AI: protect productivity and learning

Participants were concerned that potential usage limits could disproportionately affect juniors, who use AI for administrative work and self-directed learning. Repeated requests for additional allowances could create another approval burden and discourage useful experimentation.

Ideas raised included understanding actual usage before allocating allowances, consulting frequent users, allocating capacity according to work requirements and providing a straightforward route to additional access.

The transcript does not establish a confirmed implementation date, allowance or final policy. Participants also acknowledged that AI can be inaccurate and that client skills, judgement and experience still require coaching and practical exposure.

Leadership response and follow-through

The senior leader acknowledged the concerns and identified AI access, strategy communication and European–US connectivity as areas for follow-up. Protecting the culture during hiring and growth was another clear priority.

The council was expected to consolidate anonymised feedback for management. However, the discussion did not establish a complete action plan with named owners and deadlines.

Recommended next steps are to:

1. Assign an accountable owner and target date to each agreed priority.
2. Start with practical improvements: support contacts, accessible training materials and clearer process responsibilities.
3. Clarify AI policy and address concerns before any limits affect established workflows.
4. Provide regular strategy updates, including when decisions remain outstanding.
5. Report back to participants on what has changed, what is still being assessed and what cannot be progressed.

The session format itself received positive feedback: small, in person, moderated and focused on listening. Further sessions should retain those features and include a broader business mix. The clearest test of success will be whether participants see action on the issues they raised.